From 87f6d3e807280a06aa4f35c5b134b68ddeea857a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 18:47:56 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] d/wmr: Fix controller exposure slot mapping, add clock
 alignment for constellation frames

Two fixes, verified live on hardware.

1. Corrects patch 0015's slot-addressing hypothesis. 0015 tried
   camera_id + tcam_count for the controller-tracking exposure/gain hardware
   slots and left it explicitly unvalidated ("the controller stream stays
   black on a G2, so either the slot mapping is wrong or the frames are not
   arriving"). Confirmed live: +tcam_count sends successfully (no USB error)
   but the frame's own embedded exposure readback stays 0 regardless,
   100% of ~2400 frames sampled. Switched to a flat +2 (thaytan's original
   constant, not scaled) since this hardware's real camera location IDs are
   0,1,4,5, not contiguous 0-3 -- +2 lands on slots 2,3,6,7 and the readback
   immediately became nonzero and controllable (confirmed 400->20, 6000->300,
   both exactly /20, a clean linear relationship). Also bumped the exposure
   and gain values themselves well above thaytan's reference (6000/100
   instead of 400/1): the reference values produced a nonzero but still
   fully black readback on this hardware. With both changes, the debug GUI's
   "Controller Blob Cam N" panels went from solid black to showing real LED
   blobs that visibly track controller movement -- and RANSAC-PnP started
   recovering real device poses (verified via
   CONSTELLATION_TRACKER_LOG=trace: "recovered pose for device 0 from 6
   blobs", sample #2070 at pos=(-0.039,-0.012,-0.130), reprojection error
   0.04-0.10px, held stable over 2000+ consecutive samples).

2. Adds hw2mono clock-domain correction for controller-tracking frames.
   The SLAM cam_sinks path (receive_cam0..3) has always applied
   "xf->timestamp += ws->cam_hw2mono" before forwarding frames, converting
   the WMR hardware's raw device clock into Monado's host monotonic clock --
   but the controller-tracking path never did, so constellation sample
   timestamps stayed in the raw hardware clock while get_tracked_pose's
   freshness check compares against os_monotonic_get_ns(). Confirmed live:
   a fixed ~44.5 million ms (~12.4 hour) mismatch, causing every constellation
   sample to fail the 200ms freshness gate and fall back to the placeholder
   position, regardless of how good the underlying pose solve was. Added
   receive_ctrl_cam, mirroring receive_cam0..3's correction, wired in front
   of the controller-tracking blobwatch chain.

   Only PARTIALLY closes the loop: confirmed the correction reaches
   CameraSample and Camera::pushPose correctly, but found a second,
   NOT-understood bug in the same run -- sample.timestamp_ns at the
   push_sample call site is consistently corrected for one device_id and
   consistently raw for another, deterministically, despite both reaching
   that exact same code path. get_tracked_pose still reports
   position_tracked=no for at least one controller as a result. Documented
   in place (t_constellation_tracker.cpp) and in docs/pruebas.jsonl T149
   for the next session to pick up.

Also fixes wmr_controller_base_get_tracked_pose's diagnostic log (added in
9c78bc346): its throttle used a function-local `static` counter shared
across every wcb instance (left AND right controller both call this
function), so it only ever fired for whichever device's calls happened to
land on the modulo-90 boundary, silently hiding the other controller's
output the entire time. This is what made the device-specific timestamp
bug above so hard to see in the first place. Gated on the device's own
sample_count instead.

Rebase note: rebased onto the published series. The published 0015 and 0083 have since
changed the code around the exposure defaults: the DEFAULT_CTRL_EXPOSURE/GAIN values below
(6000/100) and the flat +2 slot replace 0015's 0x0190/1 and +tcam_count, and the
WMR_CONTROLLER_CAM_EXPOSURE_US/GAIN override of 0083 still works on top. The
receive_ctrl_cam container_of misuse in this patch is fixed by 0017, which must go in
together with it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
